</paragraphs>
        </title>
        <slides environment="" chapter_type="">
          <slide environment="" chapter_type="Week" canon_num="3" scope="section" id="s3" slide="3" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" chapter_title="10" section_title="More on Ideals">
            <keywords slide="3" environ="slide" chapter="10" course="Math 2078">
              <keyword chapter="10" slide="3">prime ideal</keyword>
            </keywords>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <title environment="title" chapter_type="Week" wbtag="title" scope="section" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" serial="" slide="3">
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="title" wbtag="paragraphs">
More on Ideals
</paragraphs>
            </title>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <statement environment="statement" chapter_type="Week" type="Definition" wbtag="defn" num="1" md5="3kyU9ZMBMvtN6B5vxHH2S8" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="3" item="10.1">
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs">
An ideal $I$ in a commutative ring $R$ is said to be a </paragraphs>
              <inline_keyword course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="keyword">prime ideal</inline_keyword>
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs"> if it is proper ($I\subsetneq R$) and $ab \in I$ implies that at least one of $a, b$ lies in $I$.
</paragraphs>
            </statement>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <comment course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="">
@prop
@col
If $k$ is a field, then a nonzero polynomial $p \in k[x]$ is irreducible if and only if $(p)$ is a prime ideal.
@end
</comment>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <statement environment="statement" chapter_type="Week" type="Proposition" wbtag="prop" num="2" md5="3YRx4hmcfDpU5NRJy7y2B0" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="3" item="10.2">
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
              <newcol course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="newcol">
                <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">
A proper ideal $I$ in a commutative ring $R$ is a prime ideal if and only if the quotient ring $R/I$ is an integral domain.
</paragraphs>
              </newcol>
            </statement>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
          </slide>
          <slide environment="" chapter_type="Week" wbtag="slide" canon_num="4" id="s4" slide="4" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" chapter_title="10" section_title="More on Ideals">
            <keywords slide="4" environ="slide" chapter="10" course="Math 2078">
              <keyword chapter="10" slide="4">maximal ideal</keyword>
            </keywords>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <statement environment="statement" chapter_type="Week" type="Definition" wbtag="defn" num="3" md5="7cLwVmh29NO0xPYJVYUIfW" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="4" item="10.3">
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs">
An ideal $I$ in a commutative ring $R$ is said to be a </paragraphs>
              <inline_keyword course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="keyword">maximal ideal</inline_keyword>
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs"> if $I \subsetneq R$ and there is no proper ideal $J$ of $R$ which properly includes $I$:
\[
I \subsetneq J \subsetneq R
\]
</paragraphs>
            </statement>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <statement environment="statement" chapter_type="Week" type="Lemma" wbtag="lemma" num="4" md5="5BB4N8jOR1Ymrqg6hJTiDo" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="4" item="10.4">
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs">
The zero ideal $\{0\}$ of a commutative ring $R$ is a maximal ideal if and only if $R$ is a field.
</paragraphs>
            </statement>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
          </slide>
          <slide environment="" chapter_type="Week" wbtag="slide" canon_num="5" id="s5" slide="5" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" chapter_title="10" section_title="More on Ideals">
            <keywords slide="5" environ="slide" chapter="10" course="Math 2078"/>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <statement environment="statement" chapter_type="Week" type="Proposition" wbtag="prop" num="5" md5="2qwpqc0RC0HC7js8gPbAUF" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="5" item="10.5">
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs">
A proper ideal $I$ in a commutative ring $R$ is a maixmal ideal if and only if the quotient ring $R/I$ is a field.
</paragraphs>
            </statement>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <statement environment="statement" chapter_type="Week" type="Corollary" wbtag="cor" num="6" md5="4nAadUlAd4QVrkN8hzuiMY" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="5" item="10.6">
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs">
Evey maximal ideal of a commutative ring is a prime ideal.
</paragraphs>
            </statement>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <statement environment="statement" chapter_type="Week" type="Example" wbtag="eg" num="7" md5="61vcNp3t3S8YOPraH0YHDm" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="5" item="10.7">
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
              <newcol course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="newcol">
                <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">
There are prime ideals which are not maximal ideals.</paragraphs>
                <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">Take $I = (x)$ in $\mathbb{Z}[x]$.
Then, $I$ is a prime ideal, but $\mathbb{Z}[x]/(x)$ is a not a field. So, $I$ is not a maximal ideal in $\mathbb{Z}$.
</paragraphs>
              </newcol>
            </statement>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <statement environment="statement" chapter_type="Week" type="Example" wbtag="eg" num="8" md5="04JBpl2G46xGCCwrXDHMOn" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="5" item="10.8">
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
              <newcol course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="newcol">
                <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">
Let $p$ be a prime.
Define an ideal $J \subsetneq I$ as follows:
\[
J 
= (x, p)
= \{f \in \mathbb{Z}[x] : p \text{ divides constant term of } f \}.
\]
Then, $\mathbb{Z}[x] / J \cong \mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}$, which is a field.
Hence, $J$ is a maximal ideal.
</paragraphs>
              </newcol>
            </statement>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
          </slide>
          <slide environment="" chapter_type="Week" wbtag="slide" canon_num="6" id="s6" slide="6" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" chapter_title="10" section_title="More on Ideals">
            <keywords slide="6" environ="slide" chapter="10" course="Math 2078"/>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <statement environment="statement" chapter_type="Week" type="Proposition" wbtag="prop" num="9" md5="0eqY60Vqct2N08YCSA39cK" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="6" item="10.9">
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs">
Let $n$ be any positive integer.
Let $k$ be a field. Then, for any $a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_n$, the ideal:
\[
(x_1 - a_1, \ldots, x_n - a_n)
\]
is a maximal ideal of $k[x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n]$.
</paragraphs>
            </statement>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <substatement environment="substatement" chapter_type="Week" type="Proof" wbtag="proof" md5="0gfTY3p0shSll9HlebONtJ" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="6" of="0eqY60Vqct2N08YCSA39cK" of-src-course="Math 2078" of-src-chapter="10" of-src-slide="6" of-type="Proposition" of-item="10.9" of-src-filename="week10.xml" ofmd5="0eqY60Vqct2N08YCSA39cK" of-serial="">
              <of-title hidden="true">Proposition 10.9</of-title>
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="substatement" wbtag="paragraphs">
We will sketch the proof for the special case where $a_1 = a_2 = \cdots = a_n = 0$.
The general case is left as an exercise.</paragraphs>
              <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="substatement"/>
              <newcol course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="newcol">
                <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">
We claim that $f(0, 0, \ldots, 0) = 0$ if and only if $f \in (x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n)$.</paragraphs>
                <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">One direction is clear: If $f \in (x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n)$,
then $f(0, 0, \ldots, 0) = 0$.</paragraphs>
                <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">For the other direction, we prove by induction on the number of variables.
The case $n = 1$ is equivalent to showing that $f \in k[x]$ has a root at $x = 0$ only if $f \in (x)$. This is true (we will prove this in a later section).</paragraphs>
                <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                <collapse course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="collapse">
                  <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">
Induction Step:</paragraphs>
                  <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                  <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">Suppose it is known that $f(0, 0, \ldots, 0)$ only if $f \in (x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_{n - 1})$ in $k[x_1, \ldots, x_{n - 1}]$.</paragraphs>
                  <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                  <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">For any $f(x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n) = \sum c_{k_1, k_2, \ldots, k_n} x_1^{k_1}x_2^{k_2}\cdots x_n^{k_n}
\in k[x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n]$, it may be rewritten as:
\begin{align*}
f(x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n) 
&amp;= \sum a_k x_n^k F_k(x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_{n - 1})
\\
&amp;= a_0 F_0(x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_{n - 1}) 
+ \sum_{k &gt; 0}a_k x_n^k F_k(x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_{n - 1}),
\end{align*}
where $F_k \in k[x_1, \ldots, x_{n - 1}]$.</paragraphs>
                  <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                  <collapse course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="collapse">
                    <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">
If $f(0, 0, \ldots, 0) = 0$, then:
\[
0 = a_0 F_0(0, 0, \ldots, 0)
\]
So, either $a_0 = 0$ or $F_0(0, \ldots, 0) = 0$.</paragraphs>
                    <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                    <collapse course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="collapse">
                      <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">
If $a_0 = 0$, then $f \in (x_n) \subseteq (x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n)$. </paragraphs>
                      <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                      <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">Otherwise, $F_0(0, 0, \ldots, 0) = 0$.
By the induction hypothesis, this implies that $F_0 \in (x_1, \ldots, x_{n - 1})$.
Hence,
\begin{align*}
f(x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n) 
&amp;= \underbrace{a_0 F_0(x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_{n - 1})}_{\in (x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_{n - 1})}
+ 
\underbrace{\sum_{k &gt; 0}a_k x_n^k F_k(x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_{n - 1})}_{ \in (x_n)}
\\
&amp;
\in (x_1, x_2 \ldots, x_n)
\end{align*}</paragraphs>
                      <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                      <collapse course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="collapse">
                        <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">
We conclude that:
$f(0, 0, \ldots, 0) = 0$ if and only if $f \in (x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n)$.</paragraphs>
                        <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                        <collapse course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="collapse">
                          <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">
Define a map:
\[
\phi : k[x_1, \ldots, x_n] \longrightarrow k
\]
as follows:
\[
\phi: f \mapsto f(0, 0, \ldots, 0).
\]
</paragraphs>
                          <collapse course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="collapse">
                            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">
Show that $\phi$ is a surjective ring homomorphism.</paragraphs>
                            <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                            <collapse course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="collapse">
                              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">
Show that $\ker \phi = (x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n)$.</paragraphs>
                              <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                              <collapse course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="collapse">
                                <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">
It now follows from the First Isomorphism Theorem that:
\[
k[x_1, \ldots, x_n]/(x_1, \ldots, x_n) \cong k.
\]
Since $k$ is a field, it now follows that $(x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n)$ is a maximal ideal.
</paragraphs>
                              </collapse>
                            </collapse>
                          </collapse>
                        </collapse>
                      </collapse>
                    </collapse>
                  </collapse>
                </collapse>
              </newcol>
            </substatement>
            <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment=""/>
          </slide>
          <slide environment="" chapter_type="Week" wbtag="slide" canon_num="7" id="s7" slide="7" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" chapter_title="10" section_title="More on Ideals">
            <keywords slide="7" environ="slide" chapter="10" course="Math 2078"/>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
The product of two ideals $A, B$ of a commutative ring $R$
is defined as follows:
\[
IJ =
\left\{
r \in R : r = \sum_{i = 1}^n a_i b_i \text{ for some }
a_i \in A, b_i \in B, n \in \mathbb{N}
\right\}
\]
</paragraphs>
            <statement environment="statement" chapter_type="Week" type="Exercise" wbtag="ex" num="10" md5="4CDAvg3ezIpy6u1sz0fgKk" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="7" item="10.10">
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs">
$AB$ is an ideal of $R$.
</paragraphs>
            </statement>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <statement environment="statement" chapter_type="Week" type="Proposition" wbtag="prop" num="11" md5="2clEQWkrTYgPrBXId15uNh" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="7" item="10.11">
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs">
Let $P$ be a prime ideal in a commutative ring $R$.
If $A$ and $B$ are ideals with $AB \subseteq P$,
then $A \subseteq P$ or $B \subseteq P$.
</paragraphs>
            </statement>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <substatement environment="substatement" chapter_type="Week" type="Remark" wbtag="remark" md5="1s1XcPa1JJrQVr21Fo5K4t" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="7">
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="substatement" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
              <newcol course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="newcol">
                <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">
This is essentially Euclid's Lemma in in the context of a commutative ring.
</paragraphs>
              </newcol>
            </substatement>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
          </slide>
          <slide environment="" chapter_type="Week" wbtag="slide" canon_num="8" id="s8" slide="8" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" chapter_title="10" section_title="More on Ideals">
            <keywords slide="8" environ="slide" chapter="10" course="Math 2078">
              <keyword chapter="10" slide="8">irreducible</keyword>
            </keywords>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <statement environment="statement" chapter_type="Week" type="Definition" wbtag="defn" num="12" md5="7wHbURncUTG8BGmRvJ5HF3" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="8" item="10.12">
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs">
Two elements $a, b$ in a commutative ring are said to be associates, if there exists a unit $u \in R$ such that:
\[
a = bu.
\]
</paragraphs>
            </statement>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <statement environment="statement" chapter_type="Week" type="Definition" wbtag="defn" num="13" md5="0X08i4uNKkNUtQS3I3dyx1" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="8" item="10.13">
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs">
An element $r$ is a commutative ring $R$ is said to be </paragraphs>
              <inline_keyword course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="keyword">irreducible</inline_keyword>
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs"> if:
</paragraphs>
              <itemize course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="itemize" wbtag="itemize">
                <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="itemize" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
                <item course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="itemize" wbtag="item">
                  <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="itemize" wbtag="paragraphs">
$r \neq 0$.
</paragraphs>
                  <item course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="itemize" wbtag="item">
                    <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="itemize" wbtag="paragraphs">
$r$ is not a unit.
</paragraphs>
                    <item course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="itemize" wbtag="item">
                      <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="itemize" wbtag="paragraphs">
$r = ab$ implies that one of $a$ or $b$ is an associate of $r$. 
</paragraphs>
                    </item>
                  </item>
                </item>
              </itemize>
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            </statement>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <statement environment="statement" chapter_type="Week" type="Example" wbtag="eg" num="14" md5="3eViqJU0LPGGVPyh6iG1Kl" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="8" item="10.14">
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs">
The set of irreducible elements in the ring $\mathbb{Z}$ is $\{\pm p : p \text{ a prime number}\}$.
</paragraphs>
            </statement>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <statement environment="statement" chapter_type="Week" type="Exercise" wbtag="ex" num="15" md5="5KSyaXknNOjqMh7d95Zh57" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="8" item="10.15">
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs">
If $r$ in a commutative ring $R$ is irreducible, then so is $rs$ for any unit $s \in R$.
</paragraphs>
            </statement>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
          </slide>
        </slides>
      </section>
      <section environment="" chapter_type="" type="Section" wbtag="section" title="Principal Ideal Domains" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" num="2" serial="10.2">
        <title environment="title" chapter_type="Week" wbtag="title" scope="section" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" serial="" slide="">
          <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="title" wbtag="paragraphs">
Principal Ideal Domains
</paragraphs>
        </title>
        <slides environment="" chapter_type="">
          <slide environment="" chapter_type="Week" canon_num="9" scope="section" id="s9" slide="9" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" chapter_title="10" section_title="Principal Ideal Domains">
            <keywords slide="9" environ="slide" chapter="10" course="Math 2078"/>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <title environment="title" chapter_type="Week" wbtag="title" scope="section" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" serial="" slide="9">
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="title" wbtag="paragraphs">
Principal Ideal Domains
</paragraphs>
            </title>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <statement environment="statement" chapter_type="Week" type="Theorem" wbtag="thm" num="16" md5="5GDMIy1rSdQnqucemJbaGO" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="9" item="10.16">
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs">
If $R$ is a PID, then every nonzero prime ideal is a maximal ideal.
</paragraphs>
            </statement>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <statement environment="statement" chapter_type="Week" type="Theorem" wbtag="thm" num="17" md5="2Ca5Y8ZRXwMwClM4gEm08x" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="9" item="10.17">
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs">
If $R$ is a PID, then $(r)$ is maximal if and only if $r$ is irreducible.
</paragraphs>
            </statement>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <comment course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="">
@cor
If $k$ is a field and $p \in k[x]$ is irreducile, then the quotient ring $k[x]/(p)$
is a field.
@end
</comment>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <statement environment="statement" chapter_type="Week" type="Corollary" wbtag="cor" num="18" md5="1uIoR0oXuCQLiIZYL8hccb" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="9" item="10.18">
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs">
If $R$ is a PID, then $p \in R$ is irreducible if and only if $(p)$ is a nonzero prime ideal.
</paragraphs>
            </statement>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
          </slide>
          <slide environment="" chapter_type="Week" wbtag="slide" canon_num="10" id="s10" slide="10" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" chapter_title="10" section_title="Principal Ideal Domains">
            <keywords slide="10" environ="slide" chapter="10" course="Math 2078"/>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <statement environment="statement" chapter_type="Week" type="Exercise" wbtag="ex" num="19" md5="3d6YEqyHDPNcwpym0sctPE" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="10" item="10.19">
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs">
If $R$ is a commutative ring and:
\[
I_1 \subseteq I_2 \subseteq \cdots \subseteq I_n
\subseteq I_{n + 1} \subseteq \cdots
\]
is an ascending chain of ideals in $R$, 
then
$J = \bigcup_{n \geq 1} I_n$ is an ideal in $R$.
</paragraphs>
            </statement>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <statement environment="statement" chapter_type="Week" type="Proposition" wbtag="prop" num="20" md5="2kCR4LyMnwQLLRQiGqErz7" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="10" item="10.20">
              <label wbtag="label" name="prop:pidchain" type="Proposition"/>
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs">
If $R$ is a PID, then for any infinitely ascending chain of ideals:
\[
I_1 \subseteq I_2 \subseteq \cdots \subseteq I_n
\subseteq I_{n + 1} \subseteq \cdots
\]
there exists $M \in \mathbb{N}$ such that $I_n = I_M$ for all $n &gt; M$:
\[
I_M = I_{M + 1} = I_{M + 2} = \cdots
\]
In other words, there can be no infinite ``strictly ascending'' chain of ideals.
</paragraphs>
            </statement>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
          </slide>
          <slide environment="" chapter_type="Week" wbtag="slide" canon_num="11" id="s11" slide="11" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" chapter_title="10" section_title="Principal Ideal Domains">
            <keywords slide="11" environ="slide" chapter="10" course="Math 2078"/>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <statement environment="statement" chapter_type="Week" type="Proposition" wbtag="prop" num="21" md5="1Wjnp6lTO1UrSslHo3Pyey" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="11" item="10.21">
              <label wbtag="label" name="prop:pidirred" type="Proposition"/>
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs">
Let $R$ be a PID. For any nonzero $r \in R$ which is not a unit, $r$ is irreducible or a product of irreducible elements.
</paragraphs>
            </statement>
            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            <substatement environment="substatement" chapter_type="Week" type="Proof" wbtag="proof" md5="4oU3eHUlqoCKSVW45Ogwco" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="11" of="1Wjnp6lTO1UrSslHo3Pyey" of-src-course="Math 2078" of-src-chapter="10" of-src-slide="11" of-type="Proposition" of-item="10.21" of-src-filename="week10.xml" ofmd5="1Wjnp6lTO1UrSslHo3Pyey" of-serial="">
              <of-title hidden="true">Proposition 10.21</of-title>
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="substatement" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
              <newcol course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="newcol">
                <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">
Let $\mathcal{S}$ be the set of ideals of $R$ generated by nonzero non-unit elements which are neither irreducible nor a product of irreducibles.</paragraphs>
                <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                <collapse course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="collapse">
                  <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">
Suppose $\mathcal{S}$ is nonempty. By </paragraphs>
                  <ref environment="newcol" chapter_type="" label="prop:pidchain" wbtag="ref" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="11" referrer-type="Proof" referrer-md5="4oU3eHUlqoCKSVW45Ogwco" src-course="Math 2078" src-chapter="10" src-slide="10" type="Proposition" item="10.20" src-filename="week10.xml" md5="2kCR4LyMnwQLLRQiGqErz7" class="lcref" serial="">
                    <title>Proposition 10.20</title>
                  </ref>
                  <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">, there exists at least one ``maximal" element $I = (r) \in \mathcal{S}$, in the following sense:</paragraphs>
                  <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                  <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">If $I$ is properly contained in another ideal $I = (r')$ (generated by some $r' \in R$), then $I' \notin \mathcal{S}$.</paragraphs>
                  <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                  <collapse course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="collapse">
                    <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">
If $(r)$ is a maximal ideal, then $r$ is irreducible, contradiction.</paragraphs>
                    <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                    <collapse course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="collapse">
                      <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">
If $(r)$ is not maximal, then there exists $a \in R$ such that:
\[
(r) \subsetneq (a) \subsetneq R
\]
</paragraphs>
                      <collapse course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="collapse">
                        <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">
This implies that $a$ is a nonzero non-unit element which is either irreducible or a product of irreducibles. Moreover, there exists $b \in R$
such that:
\[
r = ab
\]
This $b$ is not a unit, for otherwise $(r) = (a)$, a contradiction.</paragraphs>
                        <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                        <collapse course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="collapse">
                          <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">
Hence, $(r) \subseteq (b) \subsetneq R$. If $(r) = (b)$, then $a$ is a unit, a contradiction.</paragraphs>
                          <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                          <collapse course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="collapse">
                            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">
So, $(r) \subsetneq (b)$, which implies that $b$ is irreducible or product of irreducibles. It follows that $r$ is a product of irreducibles, a contradiction.</paragraphs>
                            <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                            <collapse course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="collapse">
                              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">
Hence, $\mathcal{S}$ is empty. We conclude that every nonzero non-unit element is either irreducible or a product of irreducibles.
</paragraphs>
                              <qed course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="qed"/>
                              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
                            </collapse>
                          </collapse>
                        </collapse>
                      </collapse>
                    </collapse>
                  </collapse>
                </collapse>
              </newcol>
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="substatement" wbtag="paragraphs">
</paragraphs>
            </substatement>
            <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="2" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment=""/>
          </slide>
        </slides>
      </section>
